<h2 class="bde-heading-506-128 bde-heading">
Why Is Jarrett Allen Paid $28m on His Extension?
</h2><div class="bde-fancy-container-506-129 bde-fancy-container">
  
  
	



<div class="bde-fancy-container-506-145 bde-fancy-container">
  
  
	



<div class="bde-f-text-506-158 bde-f-text">
<p>Allen is paid <strong>$28m</strong> because a center who rebounds and finishes at the rim holds his value into his late twenties, and Cleveland extended him a year early to avoid the market.</p>
<ul>
<li>His previous contract paid a flat <strong>$20m</strong> for five straight seasons.</li>
<li>He went 22nd in 2017, so the rookie scale paid him only <strong>$10.03m</strong> across four years.</li>
<li>He met the starter criteria on that rookie deal, which raised his qualifying offer from <strong>$5.66m</strong> to <strong>$7.71m</strong>.</li>
<li>He was an All-Star in 2022 and has led the league in field goal percentage.</li>
<li>He plays alongside Mobley, so Cleveland pays two frontcourt players properly at once.</li>
</ul>
<p>Cleveland has now paid him <strong>$131.91m</strong> of his <strong>$138.03m</strong> career total.</p>

